{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner continuation of R4 review, 2026-10-04: the complete 1,075-line git/MCP/CLI/UI audit at immutable 2f83bf8c75c7fec706cd2310eeef5c986da0e2bb has now been read. Its identity is 202,541 bytes, SHA-256 bd44d729a3cfa146a447b5742391ba18801658485959a3ed4db7499bdd08f74d. This adds four focused groups to my earlier main/catalogue reading 48583c65. The two other audit documents remain unread. Complete document reading is not complete semantic verification of every source test. Actual bounded body reads below use historical d691e6e9; no tests, installs, provider calls, source edits or performance execution were performed.\n\n1. Rewrite rows need replacement expectations, not just a new owner/key. The current rows still say parallel preparation re-prepares after main moves (landing:173,269), only a complete inventory settles (workspaces:976,990,1350,1412), and fork creations/mints that apply late are found and closed (workspaces:695,727,809). Preserve the useful unknown-effect/owned-cleanup invariant but write its new oracle: aggregate inventory can identify owned resources and cleanup duties; it cannot settle an unknown physical create/mint request. A definite authenticated answer or an applicable fence must settle that operation, with its own dispatch identity. Known-token expiry is a different question. Base movement invalidates the chosen manifest and requires the new review/selection behavior agreed in R2/R3; old automatic re-preparation is not carried over by default. Hold ending, reporting, responsibility and credential cleanup are separate. The deadline does not prove a dispatched process or remote request ended. Use the pending R2/R3 corrected contracts, and mark any unresolved oracle as a planning gap rather than inventing a passing old rule.\n\n2. Explicit successor witnesses for useful staging properties. Every gitops:277-493 row is Remove for the old log publisher. Retire that log mechanism. However the actual bodies at gitops:277-355 and 422-505 protect useful properties: bounded input before Git; a complete object closure rather than commit-ID alone; bytes checked by object ID/type/size; partial offsets; lost append or hash-object replies recovered from retained bytes; corrupt or excess bytes never treated as a valid object. O15 currently states only IDs and offsets. Expand or qualify it, and map the relevant properties to the new public Git staging/checkpoint boundary in I3/C2. The replacement need not recreate stageLog, its cohort-reset convention, or its redundant fixture/model-response equality. A small corrupt-object and interrupted-transfer table can prove the selected new implementation's actual resumability and closure. Distinguish local content-addressed file reconciliation from an unknown external request; local object existence is no generic permission to repeat provider side effects.\n\n3. Keep the causal distinction in the browser witness. The actual acts-screen:438 body at d691 explicitly throws a malformed-envelope bad-request refusal; its audit title is broader than that body. Rewrite it as that narrow, proven-before-admission case. A generic thrown error or absence of an entry is insufficient to show no preparation effects. The adjacent actual bodies 451-518 correctly exercise admitted-with-lost-answer, exact kept bytes, repeated delivery when unseen, and blocking a newly signed act while unresolved. Use a compact table including the narrowed refusal, unknown dispatch, exact-byte settlement after key expiry/revocation, and no admitted entry with outstanding pre-staging duties. Align its displayed statuses with R2 revision 4 and R3 revision 3 after their bytes are read/adopted; this reading grants neither adoption. Replace old lease/generation/member-wide restrictions with the actual full scope incarnation, hold instance and signer/controller rules. Deleting fixed Needs-you screen tests does not delete the two-person/browser requirement: the new journey must still show why attention belongs to this member, and a different member must not receive their private task controls or device credentials.\n\n4. Remove brittle index/upgrade assertions while owning the useful work bound. The actual fork-tokens:825-868 body mixes obsolete schema adoption with EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN index-name checks and no-temp-sort assertions. That exact test should be removed. Its retirement must not erase the separate new-model constraint that a bounded reconciliation pass does bounded work when many unrelated or not-yet-due duties exist. Identify the successor in O5 and the I3/I6 workload plan: bound records read/processed, writes and provider dispatches for a due pass; keep unknown rows; preserve unrelated lanes' progress. Do not require an old table/index name or infer bounded scans from LIMIT alone. Prefer counted operations with a small deterministic fixture, with actual query/storage cost reserved for the already proposed workload measurement, instead of another repeated timing-heavy test.\n\nShared fixtures should save setup without sharing mutable scope rows, clocks, faults, filesystem destinations or cleanup duties. Reuse immutable repository objects and fixture constructors; each mutating scenario gets its own state and explicit cleanup. A test comparing a real adapter to the same fake used to generate its expectations is not, by itself, an independent safety oracle. Map each retained/replacement assertion to the boundary that can actually violate it, and avoid rerunning the same pure table under every app runner merely to fill old test counts.\n\nThe complete per-test review remains open. This is design feedback under original R4 request b18554a6 / promise f3c5d607 and direction d4e07064, not a new implementation commission, audit approval, performance claim, compatibility requirement or additional Jam readiness gate. R4 stays unfiled under the separate sprint coordination.","body":{"head":"2f83bf8c75c7fec706cd2310eeef5c986da0e2bb","scope":"R4 design-only; complete git/MCP/CLI/UI audit document read; bounded historical source-body checks","source_execution":"none","verdict":"corrections requested; other two audit documents unread"}}